"udisksd runs as root to automatically detect and mount block devices. It needs the usb_storage kernel module to enumerate USB mass\u2011storage devices, expose them as /dev/sd* nodes, and trigger its automount policies. Without this module it cannot see USB sticks, so the workload keeps the surface open to provide transparent USB storage handling.\n\nThe usb_storage module (weight\u202f4.5) links to one reachable CVE in the usb\u2011parsing cluster. Exploiting this flaw triggers a heap\u2011based buffer overflow when parsing malicious USB device descriptors, giving an attacker arbitrary kernel code execution and privilege escalation from user space to root.\n\nAdd a udev rule that sets ENV{UDISKS_IGNORE}=1 for all USB storage devices; udisksd will skip probing them, allowing the usb_storage module to be unloaded while udisks2 still handles optical discs or other block devices. If automatic USB mounting is required, no substitute exists and the trade\u2011off is loss of that automation, requiring manual mount or a less privileged userspace mounter."